    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar.jpg" alt="Simón Bolívar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sim%C3%B3n_Bol%C3%ADvar._Toro_Moreno,_Luis._1922,_Legislative_Palace,_La_Paz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Luis Enrique Toro Moreno (1897-1933), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Venezuela emerged as a separate republic from the break-up of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Gran</a> Colombia. The colonial Captaincy General of Venezuela, birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>, had been the first Spanish American territory to declare full independence, on 5 July 1811, but its first two republics were crushed by royalist forces, and independence was secured only within Gran Colombia after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carabobo">Battle of Carabobo</a> in 1821. Venezuelan elites resented government from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, and in 1826 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a>, the llanero general who commanded in Venezuela, defied the central government in the revolt known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-cosiata">La Cosiata</a>. In November 1829 assemblies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> and other towns called for separation, and in 1830 a constituent congress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, convened under Páez, proclaimed Venezuela a sovereign state and in September adopted a constitution, with Caracas as the capital. The congress was openly hostile to Bolívar, who died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> that December. Páez became the first constitutional president in 1831 and dominated the country for nearly two decades. The new state was poor and thinly populated, with perhaps 800,000 inhabitants, and its war-damaged economy depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> survived until 1854, and many independence leaders became a new landed elite.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cabimas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cabimas.jpg" alt="Cabimas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cabimas">Cabimas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Autopista_lara_zulia_en_cabimas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zulianito, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For a century after independence Venezuela was an agrarian country exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and hides, with a society of large landowners, a majority of mixed-race pardos, smaller numbers of indigenous people and, until 1854, enslaved Africans. Coffee from the Andes and the central highlands became the leading export in the nineteenth century, and its prices dictated the fortunes of governments. Everything changed with oil. Commercial production began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zumaque-i">Zumaque I</a> well at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mene-grande">Mene Grande</a> in 1914, and the blowout of the Barroso II well near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabimas">Cabimas</a> in 1922 revealed the vast reserves of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-maracaibo">Lake Maracaibo</a> basin. By 1928 Venezuela was the world’s largest oil exporter and second only to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as a producer. Oil revenue built roads, schools and modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, but agriculture declined and millions moved to the cities. Immigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Portugal and Colombia arrived in large numbers after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Dependence on oil, which came to provide more than nine-tenths of export earnings, made the country highly vulnerable to swings in price. Venezuela holds the world’s largest proven oil reserves, mostly the heavy crude of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco-belt">Orinoco Belt</a>. Its population, about 28 million in 2025 after mass emigration, is largely urban, Catholic and of mixed descent.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/federal-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federal-war.jpg" alt="Federal War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/federal-war">Federal War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Combate_de_Maiquetia,_el_2_de_septiembre_de_1859.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nineteenth-century Venezuela was a land of caudillos whose private armies of llaneros and peasants decided who governed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolution-of-the-reforms">Revolution of the Reforms</a> of 1835 briefly overthrew the civilian president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-vargas">José María Vargas</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-war">Federal War</a> of 1859–1863, fought between Liberal federalists and Conservative centralists, was the bloodiest civil conflict of the century; the Liberal leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezequiel-zamora">Ezequiel Zamora</a> was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-carlos-pangasinan">San Carlos</a> in 1860, and the Treaty of Coche ended the war in 1863. Civil wars and revolts continued until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cipriano-castro">Cipriano Castro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-vicente-gomez">Juan Vicente Gómez</a>, leaders from the Andean state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tachira">Táchira</a>, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> in 1899. Castro’s refusal to pay foreign debts led to a naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1902–1903. Gómez created a professional national army, financed by oil revenue, that ended the era of regional caudillos. The armed forces remained central to politics, taking part in the coups of 1945 and 1948 and ruling until 1958. Venezuela has fought no major foreign war since independence, but it maintains a long-standing claim to the Essequibo region administered by Guyana, which an arbitral award of 1899 had rejected. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-chavez">Hugo Chávez</a>, an army officer who led a failed coup in 1992, expanded the military’s role in government, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-maduro">Nicolás Maduro</a> officers came to control ministries, oil and food distribution.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez.jpg" alt="José Antonio Páez" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_J._Peoli_-_General_Jose_Antonio_Paez_-_1910.10.4_-_Smithsonian_American_Art_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John J. Peoli, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Venezuela has had some twenty-six constitutions, among the most in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">Páez</a> and the Conservative oligarchy ruled from 1830 to 1847, followed by the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-tadeo-monagas">José Tadeo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gregorio-monagas">José Gregorio Monagas</a>, whose Liberal regime abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1854. The 1864 constitution created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of Venezuela, a federation of states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-guzman-blanco">Antonio Guzmán Blanco</a>, who dominated from 1870 to 1888, decreed free and compulsory primary education, introduced the bolívar as the currency, curbed the Church and rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> in a French style. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-vicente-gomez">Juan Vicente Gómez</a> ruled as dictator from 1908 to 1935, centralising power and using oil wealth to pay off the foreign debt. After his death the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleazar-lopez-contreras">Eleazar López Contreras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-medina-angarita">Isaías Medina Angarita</a> began a cautious opening, and the Hydrocarbons Law of 1943 raised the state’s share of oil profits. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trienio-adeco">Trienio</a> of 1945–1948 the Democratic Action party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulo-betancourt">Rómulo Betancourt</a> introduced universal suffrage, and the novelist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulo-gallegos">Rómulo Gallegos</a> was elected president in 1947, only to be overthrown in 1948. After the dictatorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcos-perez-jimenez">Marcos Pérez Jiménez</a> fell in January 1958, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punto-fijo-pact">Punto Fijo</a> pact between the main parties produced four decades of elected government under the 1961 constitution. The 1999 constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-chavez">Hugo Chávez</a> created the Bolivarian Republic with a single-chamber <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/baghdad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baghdad.jpg" alt="Baghdad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:5628442718_b10fc2c47f_o.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">USACE HQ , JIM GORDAN, CIV, USACE, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Venezuela’s influence has rested on oil and on the legacy of Bolívar. As the world’s leading oil exporter for decades from the late 1920s, it supplied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and pioneered the bargaining power of producers: its 1943 law and the fifty-fifty profit-sharing principle adopted in 1948 were copied across the oil world, and its minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-pablo-perez-alfonzo">Juan Pablo Pérez Alfonzo</a> was a founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">Organisation of the Petroleum Exporting Countries</a> (OPEC) at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1960. The nationalisation of 1976 created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petroleos-de-venezuela">Petróleos de Venezuela</a> (PDVSA), for a time one of the world’s largest oil companies. From 1958 to the 1990s Venezuela was one of the most stable democracies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulo-betancourt">Betancourt</a>’s doctrine of refusing recognition to governments that seized power by force influenced regional diplomacy. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-chavez">Chávez</a>, Venezuela led a bloc of left-wing governments, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivarian-alliance-for-the-peoples-of-our-america">Bolivarian Alliance for the Peoples of Our America</a> (ALBA) with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 2004 and supplying subsidised oil to Cuba and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrocaribe">Petrocaribe</a>. Venezuelan culture has reached a wide audience through the novels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulo-gallegos">Rómulo Gallegos</a>, the youth orchestra programme <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-sistema">El Sistema</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-abreu">José Antonio Abreu</a> in 1975, which inspired similar projects worldwide, and its many players in American baseball. Since 2015 the exodus of nearly eight million Venezuelans has become one of the largest displacement crises in the world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/caracazo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caracazo.jpg" alt="Caracazo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/caracazo">Caracazo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Una_v%C3%ADctima_del_Caracazo_en_Petare.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yrmauhili, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Punto Fijo democracy, built on oil revenue, entered crisis in the 1980s. Falling prices and debt forced a devaluation on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viernes-negro">Black Friday</a> in February 1983, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-andres-perez">Carlos Andrés Pérez</a> introduced austerity measures in 1989, the riots known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracazo">Caracazo</a> were suppressed with hundreds killed. Lieutenant Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-chavez">Hugo Chávez</a> led a failed coup in February 1992, and Pérez was impeached in 1993. Chávez won the presidency in December 1998, and the 1999 constitution renamed the state the Bolivarian Republic of Venezuela. He survived a brief coup in April 2002 and an oil strike in 2002–2003, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petroleos-de-venezuela">PDVSA</a>, and used high oil prices to fund social programmes and nationalisations. After his death in 2013 his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-maduro">Nicolás Maduro</a> faced collapsing oil prices and production, hyperinflation and severe shortages; the economy shrank by roughly three-quarters between 2013 and 2020, and millions emigrated. Maduro governed by increasingly authoritarian means, sidelining the opposition-controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> elected in 2015 and claiming victory in the disputed elections of 2018 and 2024, results the opposition and many foreign governments rejected. In January 2026 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forces captured Maduro in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> to face drug-trafficking charges, and Vice-President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delcy-rodriguez">Delcy Rodríguez</a> assumed the presidency. Venezuela remains a federal presidential republic with Caracas as its capital, its political future uncertain.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Venezuela, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> that left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Gran</a> Colombia in 1830, was transformed by the world’s largest oil reserves from an impoverished land of caudillos into a founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a> and a stable democracy, before the Bolivarian revolution and economic collapse made it the centre of a regional crisis.</em></p>

